# Role-based API access

Complements `platform-patterns.mdc` (auth wiring), `multi-tenant-safety.mdc` (tenant scope), `rest-api-convention.mdc` (path prefixes), `response-contracts.mdc` (no field leakage).

**Frontend role checks are UX only.** The API is the authority.

## Purpose

- Employees must not call admin or client-only endpoints.
- Clients must not call `/admin` or `/employee`.
- A user token must never read or mutate **another** user’s private resources (IDOR).

Auth middleware is not shipped yet — still design routes and services to these rules so wiring auth is additive, not a rewrite.

## Defense in depth

```txt
router mount (audience) → requireAuth → requireRole / requirePermission → controller → service (object scope)
```

| Layer | Responsibility |
| ----- | -------------- |
| Path prefix | `/admin`, `/employee`, `/me`, app resources — wrong surface is obvious |
| `requireAuth` | Valid session/token; sets `req.user` |
| `requireRole` / `requirePermission` | Role gate; throw `ForbiddenError` (403) |
| Service | Object-level checks: owner, membership, tenant — never trust body/params alone |

```ts
// routes — middleware only here
adminRouter.use(requireAuth, requireRole("admin"));
employeeRouter.use(requireAuth, requireRole("employee"));
meRouter.use(requireAuth);
brandsRouter.use(requireAuth); // client app; add requirePermission per route if needed
```

Do **not** add stub `requireAuth()` that always passes (`platform-patterns.mdc`).

## Role → surface matrix

| Role | May call | Must not call |
| ---- | -------- | ------------- |
| Client / customer | App resources, `/me/*` | `/admin/*`, `/employee/*` |
| Employee (staff) | `/employee/*` (+ shared read-only if explicitly allowed) | `/admin/*`, other users’ `/me`-shaped private data via client routes |
| Admin | `/admin/*` | Impersonation without audit; never “skip” object checks |

Prefer **separate routers** per audience over one fat router with scattered `if (role)` branches.

## Object-level authorization (IDOR)

Every read/write by id must prove the actor may access **that** row.

```ts
// ❌ IDOR — any authenticated user with a guessed UUID
const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id: req.params.userId } });

// ❌ Trusting client-supplied owner
await updateProfile({ userId: body.userId, ...body });

// ✅ Self — id from token only
await updateProfile({ userId: req.user!.id, ...body });

// ✅ Member lookup — scope + existence hiding
const row = await prisma.order.findFirst({
  where: { id, organizationId: req.organization!.id },
});
if (!row) throw new NotFoundError("Order not found"); // same for wrong-tenant
```

Rules:

1. **Self resources** — use `/me` and `req.user.id`; ignore `userId` in body/query/params as authority.
2. **Never** authorize solely because the path matched (`/users/:userId` ≠ permission).
3. Wrong-tenant / wrong-owner → `NotFoundError` (404), not 403 that confirms existence.
4. Controllers pass actor ids into services; services never read `req` (`platform-patterns.mdc`).

## Tokens and cross-user access

| Check | Rule |
| ----- | ---- |
| Session user | From verified token/session only |
| `userId` in body/query | Not authoritative |
| Admin acting on a user | Explicit `/admin/...` + permission + audit log |
| Employee support lookup | `/employee/...` + permission + audit; least privilege |

## Good / bad

```ts
// ✅ employee cannot reach admin mount
apiRouter.use("/admin", requireAuth, requireRole("admin"), adminRouter);

// ✅ client brands scoped in service
listBrands({ organizationId: req.organization!.id, query });

// ❌ role check only in controller after heavy work
// ❌ GET /users/:userId/orders for "my orders"
// ❌ requireRole("admin") on some admin routes but not all under /admin
```

## Explicit prohibitions

- No security by frontend route guards alone.
- No shared “god” endpoints usable by every role with different filters.
- No `findUnique({ where: { id } })` on private rows without actor/tenant scope.
- No returning other users’ data because “they’re both authenticated.”
- No role checks inside services that should be route middleware (keep services framework-agnostic; pass `actorId` / flags).

## AI guidance

New endpoint: pick audience prefix (`rest-api-convention.mdc`), attach `requireAuth` + role/permission on the router, pass actor/tenant ids into the service, scope every query. Review diffs for IDOR (param id without actor check) before merge. Tenant tables: also `multi-tenant-safety.mdc`.
